spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:3:- **B-1 — High — API keys remain in React 
Query’s mutation cache.**  
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:5:  Saving passes the full `apiKey` to 
`mutate()`. TanStack retains it in `mutation.state.variables` after success or failure; clearing the password field 
does not remove it. An installed-library probe confirmed this retention. The shared cache survives sign-out, exposing 
the old key through mutation inspection, including development query tools. The existing canary E2E checks responses, 
input values, and HTML, but never mutation state.  
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:6:  **Fix:** keep the write-only key outside 
cached mutation variables, clear secret-bearing state on settlement and identity changes, and extend the canary E2E to 
check retention before reloading.
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:10:  The mutation supplies no cancellation signal 
or user/company fence. Its hook-level `onSuccess` always calls `saveFile()`. Start an export containing a transcript, 
delay the response, then sign out and sign into another company: the old response can still create a download in the 
new session. A Query Core probe confirmed that this callback executes after its observer unsubscribes. Other note 
mutations also retain callbacks and cached results after scope retirement.  
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:11:  **Fix:** capture the originating identity, 
abort pending requests on scope retirement, reject late callbacks, and clear the retired scope’s query and mutation 
data. Add an E2E covering a delayed export across account switching.
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:15:  A failed save invalidates the detail query. 
If the browser remains offline, that refetch fails; `detail.isError` then replaces the editor before the `editing` 
branch, unmounting its draft state. The installed Query Core reproduces an error result while retaining previous data, 
so this happens despite having a usable cached note. Reconnecting cannot recover the discarded text.  
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:16:  **Fix:** preserve the editor and draft 
through transport/server refetch failures and show an error alongside it. Add an offline-save E2E asserting that every 
edited field survives reconnection.
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:18:- **B-4 — Medium — Confirmation dialogs 
silently adopt newer revisions.**  
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:20:  Delete and Restore read the live prop’s 
revision when confirmation is clicked. Open Delete at revision 3, let another editor change the content, then return 
after a production focus refetch: confirmation sends revision 4 and deletes the concurrent work without a 409. The 
dialog can remain visually identical because it shows only the title. Restore and item deletion have the same 
revision-adoption problem.  
spec-interview/phase-6-meeting-notes/reviews/sol-review-blockB-r1.md:21:  **Fix:** capture the revision and displayed 
state when the operation is initiated. Require explicit review before adopting a conflict’s newer revision. Cover 
background refetch during confirmation in E2E.
